    "display_name": "C19 - Panic-Bid: financials/energy shock reversion · ranked by lowest volatility",
    "discipline": "volatility",
    "purpose": "trading",
    "thesis": "A close below the 20-day, 2-sigma Bollinger lower band, with RSI(14) under 30 and the day's true range exceeding its own 14-day ATR, is three independent confirmations of the same event: a statistically extreme, accelerating one-day dislocation, not an ordinary pullback inside a trend. In financials and energy specifically -- the two sectors whose largest single-day moves have repeatedly been forced selling rather than a revision of fundamentals (margin calls, fund redemptions, program-driven de-grossing, a headline that gets re-priced within days once it is understood) -- that kind of move tends to partially retrace over the following one to three weeks, because the sellers on the shock day are disproportionately non-economic: risk desks cutting exposure into a VaR breach, index/ETF-driven flows, and retail panic, not new information about ten years of future cash flows for JPMorgan or Exxon. The counterparty on the other side of my buy is exactly that forced seller -- someone selling because a risk limit or a margin call requires it that day, not because they have re-underwritten the business -- and the person I hand the position to on exit is whoever is chasing the bounce once the panic is visibly over. This is a volatility-discipline entry, not a dip-buy dressed up as one: the ATR-relative range condition is what is missing from a plain RSI/Bollinger mean-reversion rule, and it is what keeps the trigger anchored to days when the market is repricing fast rather than merely drifting to an oversold reading over two quiet weeks. The strategy is a genuine short-vol / mean-reversion payoff, not a convex one: it harvests a moderate, capped reversion after each shock and is capped on the downside by a stop, but a stop checked against the prior close (not the intraday low, per the engine's execution model) means a shock that is the start of a structural decline rather than a one-day panic -- oil in the second half of 2014 is the clearest historical case in this universe -- can gap through the stop before it is caught, which is this strategy's real tail risk and is stated here before any result exists, not fitted after seeing one.",
